{"result":"{
  "metadata": {
    "id": "400195600",
    "title": "04.71 Writing User Stories for Components",
    "type": "page",
    "created": "",
    "updated": "",
    "url": "https://zimmermannworkboard.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SFCC/pages/400195600",
    "space": {
      "key": "SFCC",
      "name": "Adobe SFCC"
    },
    "version": 2,
    "attachments": [],
    "content": {
      "value": "### How to write user stories for the Zimmermann build\n\n#### Overview\n\nUser stories are organised across two levels: component epics and page epics. Each serves a different purpose and audience.\n\n---\n\n#### Level 1 — Component epics\n\nThis is the build story for each individual component. It covers what the component does, its variants and configurations, accessibility requirements, and any error or empty states.\n\n**There are two types of component, and this determines how many stories you write:**\n\n**PWA Kit only** (e.g. Button, Textfield, Dropdown, Cart Item)  \nThese are functional UI components with no content authoring required. Write one story, in the PWA Kit epic only. No Contentful story is needed.\n\n**Contentful + PWA Kit** (e.g. Banner, Editorial Cards, Footer, Navigation)  \nThese components have content that an editor manages. Write two stories:\n\n* One in the **Contentful epic** — covering the content model, authoring fields, configuration options and validation rules\n* One in the **PWA Kit epic** — covering how the component renders and responds to what Contentful delivers\n\n> A Contentful component always needs a PWA Kit story too, because PWA Kit has to render it. A PWA Kit-only component does not need a Contentful story.\n\n---\n\n#### Level 2 — Page epics\n\nThis is the experience story for a specific page (e.g. PDP, PLP, Checkout). Rather than focusing on individual component builds, these stories describe how components work together to deliver a user journey.\n\nWrite these from the customer's perspective — for example, on the PDP: a customer selects a size using the size selector, selects a colour using the colour attribute component, and adds the item to their bag using the add to cart component.\n\nThese stories should not repeat the component build detail from Level 1. They are about the interaction between components and the end-to-end experience on that page.\n\n---\n\n#### Quick reference\n\n| Level | Epic | Focus | Written from |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| 1 | PWA Kit component | Build, variants, accessibility | Developer perspective |\n| 1 | Contentful component | Content model, authoring, field config | Merchandiser perspective |\n| 1 | PWA Kit (render) | Rendering Contentful output | Developer perspective |\n| 2 | Page (e.g. PDP) | How components work together | Shopper perspective |",
      "format": "markdown"
    }
  }
}"}

{"result":"{
  "metadata": {
    "id": "261881857",
    "title": "Component Override Strategy",
    "type": "page",
    "created": "",
    "updated": "",
    "url": "https://zimmermannworkboard.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SFCC/pages/261881857",
    "space": {
      "key": "SFCC",
      "name": "Adobe SFCC"
    },
    "version": 4,
    "attachments": [],
    "content": {
      "value": "How Zimmermann customisation works on top of the base Salesforce PWA Kit template. For general naming/testing/styling conventions, see the parent [Development Standards](https://zimmermannworkboard.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SFCC/pages/261750785) page.\n\nThe overrides pattern\n---------------------\n\nThis project is a **slim overrides project** on top of `@salesforce/retail-react-app` v10.\n\nThe base template lives in `node_modules/@salesforce/retail-react-app/`. It cannot and must not be edited — changes there would be wiped out by the next `npm install`. All Zimmermann customisations live in `overrides/app/`, which the PWA Kit module resolution layer checks before falling back to the base template. For example:\n\nDefaultnonenode\\_modules/@salesforce/retail-react-app/app/components/product-tile/index.jsx\n↑ base template — never touch this\noverrides/app/components/product-tile/index.tsx\n↑ Zimmermann override — this is what runs in production\n\nIf a file exists in `overrides/app/`, it replaces the base template version entirely. If it does not exist in `overrides/app/`, the base template version runs.\n\nOverriding vs Creating a new file\n---------------------------------\n\n### A. Overriding a base component\n\nUse this when changing a component that already exists in the base template (e.g. `product-tile`, `header`, `checkout`).\n\n1. **Copy the file** — copy the base file to the mirrored path under `overrides/`, convert it to TypeScript, and add a temporary `console.log` to confirm the override is rendering: Defaultbashcp node\\_modules/@salesforce/retail-react-app/app/components/product-tile/index.jsx \\\n   overrides/app/components/product-tile/index.tsx\n2. Convert the file to TypeScript (`.js` → `.ts`/`.tsx`), and update import references within the new file.\n3. Run `npx tsc --noEmit` to confirm no TypeScript errors.\n\nTo test the override is working: add `console.log('[overrides] product-tile/index.tsx')` near the top, start the dev server, open the relevant page, and confirm the log fires in the browser console. Remove the `console.log` and make functional changes only after the override is confirmed rendering.\n\n**Keep the filename identical** (kebab-case) — overrides of Salesforce files keep the upstream filename exactly.\n\n### B. Creating a new Zimmermann-specific component\n\nUse this when building something that doesn't exist in the base template (e.g. a \"Back In Stock\" button, a loyalty points badge).\n\nNew custom components go under `overrides/app/components/custom/`, e.g.:\n\nDefaultnoneoverrides/app/components/custom/backInStockButton/\nindex.tsx ← the component\nindex.stories.tsx ← Storybook story (required)\nindex.test.ts ← Vitest (required if the component has non-trivial logic)\n\nNaming: `camelCase` for Zimmermann-authored folders and files (e.g. `backInStockButton/`, `useWishlist.ts`, `formatPrice.ts`).\n\nBranch first\n------------\n\nEvery change goes on a feature branch — never directly on `develop` or `main`.\n\nDefaultbashgit checkout develop\ngit pull origin develop\ngit checkout -b feature/SFCC-123\\_short-description\n\nBranch naming: `feature/{JIRA-KEY}_{short-description}`. Full branching model: see the [Branching Guide (GitFlow)](https://zimmermannworkboard.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SFCC/pages/304840733) page under Development Standards & Tooling.",
      "format": "markdown"
    },
    "emoji": "1f680",
    "page_width": "fixed-width"
  }
}"}

{"result":"{
  "metadata": {
    "id": "261750785",
    "title": "Development Standards",
    "type": "page",
    "created": "",
    "updated": "",
    "url": "https://zimmermannworkboard.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SFCC/pages/261750785",
    "space": {
      "key": "SFCC",
      "name": "Adobe SFCC"
    },
    "version": 2,
    "attachments": [],
    "content": {
      "value": "General day-to-day coding conventions for the Zimmermann PWA Kit. For the pattern used to override a base Salesforce component vs. build a new Zimmermann-specific one, see [Component Override Strategy](https://zimmermannworkboard.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SFCC/pages/261881857).\n\nNaming conventions\n------------------\n\n| What | Convention | Example |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| New Zimmermann files and folders | `camelCase` | `backInStockButton/`, `useWishlist.ts` |\n| Overrides of Salesforce base files | Keep upstream kebab-case exactly | `product-tile/index.tsx` |\n| Component named export | Matches folder name | `BackInStockButton` exported from `backInStockButton/` |\n| Component props type | Component name + `Props` | `BackInStockButtonProps` |\n| Hook files | `.ts` (not `.tsx`) | `useWishlist.ts` |\n| Hook export | Matches filename | `useWishlist` |\n| Callback props | `onEventName` | `onAddToCart`, `onClose` |\n| Identifiers and copy | Australian spelling | `colour`, `centre`, `organisation` |\n| Override files | Keep upstream Salesforce spelling verbatim | — |\n\nTypeScript only inside `overrides/`. No `.js` or `.jsx` files. Run `npx tsc --noEmit` before finishing any task that touches `overrides/`.\n\nNo `any` without an inline comment explaining why a narrower type is impossible.\n\nPrefer `type` over `interface` for component props and shared shapes.\n\nDesign tokens\n-------------\n\nDesign tokens (colours, typography, spacing) live in `overrides/app/theme/foundation/` — **frozen (Tier 1)**, owned by the UI Designer. Do not modify except to sync with Figma; ask the UI Designer if a needed colour/font isn't in the token set.\n\nDefaulttsx// ✅ correct — uses the token\n<Text color=\"brand.primary\" fontSize=\"md\">…</Text>\n// ❌ wrong — hardcoded value\n<Text color=\"#1a1a1a\" fontSize=\"16px\">…</Text>\n\nPer-component theme overrides (button variant, link styles, etc.) live in `overrides/app/theme/components/` — Tier 2 (protected), owned by the UI Designer.\n\nCommon shared components\n------------------------\n\n| Component | Path | Notes |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Header | `overrides/app/components/header/` | Tier 2 — coordinate with both squads before touching |\n| Page container | `overrides/app/components/pageContainer/` | Wraps page-level layout |\n| App config | `overrides/app/components/_app-config/` | Tier 2 |\n| Button | `overrides/app/components/button/` | Zimmermann button override |\n\nFor icons, use `@salesforce/retail-react-app/app/components/icons`. If a required icon is missing, get it from Figma or ask the UI Designer — do not substitute a visually different one.\n\nStyling\n-------\n\n1. Chakra UI props first.\n2. `.scss` file alongside the component only when Chakra props cannot achieve the required result.\n\nNever hardcode hex colours, pixel spacing values, or raw font sizes in component props.\n\nStorybook\n---------\n\nEvery component exported from `overrides/app/components/` or `overrides/app/pages/` must have a `.stories.tsx` file.\n\n| Category | What goes here |\n| --- | --- |\n| `Foundation/` | Design token visualisations |\n| `Elements/` | Generic building blocks (Button, Input, Badge) |\n| `Commerce/` | Commerce-specific components (ProductTile, OrderSummary) |\n| `Layout/` | Global shell (Header, Footer, DrawerMenu) |\n| `Forms/` | Form assemblies (AddressForm, CheckoutContactForm) |\n| `Pages/` | Full page assemblies (PDP, PLP, Cart) |\n\nEvery story file needs a `Default` named export. Add named exports for meaningful states (`Loading`, `OutOfStock`) — not for every prop combination.\n\nTesting\n-------\n\n| Layer | Tool | When |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Unit | Vitest | Data mapping, transforms, hook logic in isolation |\n| Component interaction | Storybook `play` | User interactions (clicks, form fills, focus) |\n| E2E | Playwright | Full user journeys across pages |\n\nVitest tests co-locate with source as `*.test.ts`.\n\nBefore opening a PR\n-------------------\n\nDefaultbashnpx tsc --noEmit # TypeScript must be clean\nnpm run lint # lint must pass\nnpm run test # unit tests must pass\n\nClaude Code's `/code-quality` skill runs a pre-PR audit of changed files; `/pr-checklist` generates a structured PR description.\n\nFile protection tiers\n---------------------\n\nNot all files are equally safe to edit.\n\n| Tier | What | Rule |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **Tier 1 — Frozen** | Design tokens, shared commerce hooks, SSR/config, CI/CD | Stop. Get owner approval before any change. |\n| **Tier 2 — Protected** | Global shell, shared product components, translations, shared hooks | PR requires a second reviewer. Notify the owner. |\n| **Unclassified** | A squad's own feature components and pages | Normal feature-branch workflow. |\n\nFull lists live in the repo's `.claude/rules/frozenFiles.md` and `.claude/rules/protectedFiles.md`.",
      "format": "markdown"
    },
    "emoji": "1f680",
    "page_width": "fixed-width"
  }
}"}

